Derive what randomized comparisons average over (@inquiry-randomized-comparisons: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "State-the-null-model-and-limits-of-an-exact-probability-comparison.lingua#r_0ACF4290939593619C55D72DC9") { r_0BA266DD53DF13150425227195
For unit i, write Y_i(P) and Y_i(Q) for the indications that would occur under the two protocols in the declared conditions. These are potential outcomes: in one actual assignment we observe only the indication under the assigned protocol. An unobserved alternative is not a missing value we can casually fill in. A toy model can stipulate both to examine a method; the same complete table is not ordinarily available from an experiment.

Assume here that each protocol is well defined, another unit's assignment does not change this unit's outcome, all outcomes are recorded, and the six pairs of potential values remain fixed while assignment varies. No additional reading noise or time response is included. The target is τ=(1/6)Σ[Y_i(Q)−Y_i(P)], the average effect on indication for these six units. It is not a population-wide claim or a comparison to true lengths.

Under either balanced design, estimate τ by the observed Q mean minus the observed P mean. With I_i the Q indicator, the estimate is D=(1/3)Σ I_iY_i(Q)−(1/3)Σ(1−I_i)Y_i(P). Each unit has E[I_i]=1/2 under both uniform complete assignment and the stipulated paired assignment. Linearity of expectation therefore gives E[D]=(1/6)ΣY_i(Q)−(1/6)ΣY_i(P)=τ. This proof does not require the six assignment indicators to be independent. It does require the stated inclusion probabilities, fixed potential responses and group sizes.

For the original teaching table, let P indications be (2,4,5,6,8,9) mm and let each Q indication exceed its P value by 2 mm. Hence τ=2 mm. Pair units (0,1), (2,3) and (4,5), using zero-based labels for the program. These pairs and their background values are stipulated before assignment; they do not represent an outcome-dependent pairing procedure. Complete uniform assignment gives 20 possible estimates, with expectation 2 mm, variance 40/9 mm² and range from −2 to 6 mm. Paired assignment gives eight estimates, with the same expectation, variance 2/3 mm² and range from 2/3 to 10/3 mm.

For the paired result, the within-pair P gaps are 2, 1 and 1 mm. Each pair contributes the common protocol effect plus either sign of its background gap, so D=2+(±2±1±1)/3 mm. The signs are independent and equally likely under this design. Their means and cross terms vanish, yielding variance (4+1+1)/9=2/3 mm². This explains the enumeration. The complete-design variance can be independently recovered by calculating all twenty squared deviations from 2 mm. Pairing helps for this stipulated table; it is not a universal theorem that any chosen blocks improve every design.

Now suppose the complete design realizes Q units (3,4,5). The observed indications are (2,4,5,8,10,11) mm, and D=29/3−11/3=6 mm. The known toy effect is still 2 mm. A large contrast in one allocation can combine the effect with pre-existing unit differences. If we had deliberately assigned the largest background units to Q, this would no longer be a realization from the claimed uniform mechanism. The arithmetic would survive, while the randomization argument would not.

**Read, check and critique:** use [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN)'s complete/block design discussion and the finite expectation reading in [Grinstead and Snell’s Introduction to Probability — CHANCE version](../sources/Introduction-to-Probability-Grinstead-Snell-Doyle.lingua#r_041A5B2D3F5CDC4C2EFED46B71). Enumerate both designs, reproduce the means and spreads, then change the unit-specific Q effects while keeping the assignment rules. Derive unbiasedness again without requiring constant effects. Explain why the complete-design realization (3,4,5) is not an allowed paired assignment. Specify how interference, omitted readings, protocol noncompliance or assignment chosen after outcomes would break the derivation's applicability.

